	"label": "Ice — surface / internal clouds and cracks",
	"longdesc": "",
	"name": "ice-sheet",
	"node_position": {
		"x": 0.0,
		"y": 0.0
	},
	"nodes": [
		{
			"color": {
				"a": 1.0,
				"b": 0.300000011920929,
				"g": 0.239999994635582,
				"r": 0.140000000596046,
				"type": "Color"
			},
			"name": "About",
			"node_position": {
				"x": 0.0,
				"y": -380.0
			},
			"parameters": {},
			"seed": -380.0,
			"size": {
				"x": 900.0,
				"y": 190.0
			},
			"text": "ice-sheet.tres is a ShaderMaterial of ice-sheet.gdshader: export it with this project's own target, File > Export material > Godot > Godot 4 ShaderMaterial (Godot 4 Standard would write a StandardMaterial3D the game refuses). The Export node writes ice-sheet_cracks.png, the map the shader adds. Edit IceResponse. The ...PNG8 groups and the nodes after them only store exact file values; leave them. Export into data/models/board/textures/terrain/ice, then Reload assets in the game's Tuning panel. The second Export node writes ice-sheet_floes.png, baked for the game and not in the preview: R joints, G where snow settles first (the game picks the snow line), B the cracks between a broken border's shards, A each shard's value. It repeats over 102 m: 6 plates and 16 floes across, so its nodes tile.",
			"title": "About this material",
			"type": "comment"
		},
